  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 AM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nSatellite imagery and recent surface observations show high clouds \ndrifting in from the west as high pressure remains overhead. It is \nnot out the question that sprinkles could occur today (mainly this \nmorning and afternoon) as more clouds advect in, but the overall \ntrend continues to be dry and gradually warmer through at least the \nfirst half of the weekend as the airmass overhead moderates. Highs \nwill be in the upper 70s area-wide today with low humidity. Winds \nshift to become southwesterly on Saturday with WAA starting to ramp \nup. Saturday will be slightly warmer and a bit humid with highs in \nthe low to mid 80s and dewpoints around 60 degrees. \n\nHot and humid by Sunday and Monday with increasing chances for rain \nand storms. Dewpoints continue to climb Sunday and Monday into the \nlow to mid 70s with west/southwest flow at the surface. Increased \nevapotranspiration from crops may also aid in locally higher \ndewpoints. High temperatures will be in the upper 80s to low 90s \nboth days with continued WAA ahead of a cold front that comes \nthrough late Monday/early Tuesday. Our forecast area will be on the \nfringes of a large upper level ridge over the central CONUS. This is \na favorable summertime set up for a potentially active pattern into \nearly next week with northwest flow aloft. Guidance is trending \ntowards our forecast area being in the 'ring of fire' with the large \nupper level ridge centered over the central CONUS and repeated \ncomplexes of storms (MCSs) that ride the periphery of the ridge. \nConsiderable uncertainty remains this far out on timing, track, and \nintensity of storms, so confidence is low. A pattern like this is \nvery difficult to forecast for more than 24 hours out, as it takes \nknowing when and where an MCS will develop to understand how it may \nevolve and where it will track. Several rounds of thunderstorms may \noccur Sunday evening through Monday, but exact details are sparse \nand subject to change. Sunday is trending mainly dry at this time, \nwith 20-30% chances for storms late in the evening. The better \nchances arrive Monday afternoon and evening ahead of and along the \napproaching cold front. I can't rule out isolated strong to severe \nstorms Sunday evening, but the better chances and more favorable \nenvironment for severe weather appears to be Monday. \n\nOur entire forecast area is included in the 15% severe \nrisk by SPC on Monday, July 27th. The GFS and ECMWF are in alignment \non a favorable synoptic setup with northwest flow aloft and an\nincreasing LLJ on Monday. A series of 500 mb shortwaves will \nmove through Monday, providing sufficient lift for storm \ndevelopment in conjunction with the cold front. Shear and \ninstability remain the key ingredients I have the least \nconfidence in this far out; with how hot and humid it will be, I\nwould not be surprised if instability reaches 3000-4000 J/kg\nSBCAPE on Sunday and Monday. Bulk shear appears to be decent \ntoo on Monday at around 30 to 40 kts. Uncertainty remains in \nexact timing, coverage, and intensity of storms. Given the \nhighly conditional setup, confidence remains low overall.\n\nTuesday's weather is uncertain, as it will depend on what happens \nlate Sunday and Monday. Ensemble guidance favors dry and seasonable \nconditions with high pressure building once again over the Great \nLakes. For now, will stick with dry conditions and highs in the mid \n80s.",
